{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>An ox or a sheep or a goat, when it is born.</b> They said in the Midrash: 'An ox or a sheep' — let an ox come and atone for the likeness of an ox, as it is said (Psalms 106:20): ״וימירו את כבודם בתבנית שור״. And so they expounded regarding the contribution for the Tabernacle, that it mentioned gold first — this is what is said (Exodus 25:3): ״זהב וכסף ונחשת״ — let the gold of the Tabernacle's contribution come and atone for the gold of the Calf; this is what Scripture says (Hosea 7:3): ״ברעתם ישמחו מלך״. And what do you see in an ox that it should be made the head of all the sacrifices? Rabbi Levi said: a parable of a matrona upon whom an evil report went forth concerning one of the great ones of the kingdom. The king investigated the matter and found no substance in the allegations. What did the king do? He made a banquet and seated her at the head of the guests, to make known that he had investigated and found no substance in the allegations. So the Holy One, blessed be He, investigated and found that it was not Israel who made the Calf, but the converts who were among them — these are the ערב רב who converted at the redemption from Egypt; they are the ones who said to Israel (Exodus 32:4): ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״. Therefore it was made the head of all the sacrifices. He mentioned 'matrona' and the intent was Israel." "<b>And he says.</b> I say by way of the Kabbalah that 'one of the great ones of the kingdom' is one of the טטראמולין of the Holy One, blessed be He, and in order to make it known that Israel did not begin that transgression, therefore He set the ox at the head of the sacrifices; and likewise Scripture set forth first (Leviticus 23:40): ״פרי עץ הדר כפת תמרים״, for the sin had been in it alone. Now Adam the first man sinned in thought and in deed: in thought through one of the great ones of the kingdom, and in deed through the פרי עץ הדר, and as the deeds of the fathers so did the children do; and just as Israel offered a calf to atone for the Calf, so Adam offered an ox to atone for his sin — this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: the ox that Adam the first man offered had one horn on its forehead. And we find one verse that joined both of them — the sin of thought and the sin of deed — as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:17): ״בכור שורו הדר לו״; it mentioned שור and joined to it הדר, and from this the prophet said (Hosea 7:3): ״ברעתם ישמחו מלך״, for just as we appease Him with פרי עץ הדר, in which the sin was, so we appease Him with the sacrifice of an ox, in which the sin was. And He mentioned above this verse (Hosea 7:1 — 'the preceding verse'): ״והמה כאדם עברו ברית״, explaining explicitly that the sin of Israel in which they rejoice before the Holy One, blessed be He, was the sin of Adam the first man. And that which the Torah rendered fit for sacrifice — these three species, ox or sheep or goat — our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ox in the merit of Abraham, as it is said (Genesis 18:7): ״ואל הבקר רץ אברהם״; sheep in the merit of Isaac (Genesis 22): ״וירא והנה איל״; goat in the merit of Jacob, as it is said (Genesis 27): ״לך נא אל הצאן וקח לי משם שני גדיי עזים טובים״ — good for you, good for your children: good for you, for through them you will receive the blessings; good for your children, for through them they will obtain atonement on the Day of Atonement. This is what Scripture says: ״שור או כשב או עז כי יולד״. This is what Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 3:15): ״מה שהיה הוא שיהיה ואשר להיות כבר היה והאלהים יבקש את נרדף״ — if a man should tell you that if Adam the first man had not sinned he would still be alive and enduring, say to him: Elijah did not sin and he is alive and enduring forever. ״ואשר להיות״ — if a man should tell you that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to revive the dead, say to him: ״כבר היה״ — it already was; He already did so through Elijah, through Elisha, and through Ezekiel. ״והאלהים יבקש את נרדף״: a righteous person pursues a righteous person, a wicked person pursues a wicked person, and the Elohim seeks the pursued one; a wicked person pursues a righteous person, a righteous person pursues a wicked person, and the Elohim seeks the pursued one. Know that this is so: Abel was pursued by Cain — what is written? (Genesis 4:4–5) ״וישע ה׳ אל הבל ואל מנחתו״, and ״אל קין ואל מנחתו לא שעה״. Noah was pursued by his generation — what is written? (Genesis 6:8) ״ונח מצא חן״, and it is written (Genesis 7:23): ״וימח את כל היקום״. Abraham was pursued by Nimrod — what is written? (Nehemiah 9:7) ״אשר בחרת באברם״. Isaac — by the Philistines — what is written? (Genesis 26:28) ״ויאמרו ראו ראינו כי היה ה׳ עמך״. Jacob — by Esau — what is written? (Psalms 135:4) ״כי יעקב בחר לו יה״. Joseph — by his brothers — what is written? (Genesis 39:2) ״ויהי ה׳ את יוסף ויהי איש מצליח״. Moses — by Pharaoh — what is written? (Psalms 106:23) ״לולי משה בחירו״. Israel — by the nations of the world — what is written? (Deuteronomy 14:2) ״ובך בחר ה׳ להיות לו לעם סגלה״. So too here: שור או כשב או עז — an ox is pursued by a lion, a sheep by a wolf, a goat by a leopard. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: you shall not bring Me a sacrifice from among the pursuers, but only from among the pursued." "<b>And it shall be seven days under its mother.</b> A parable of a king who entered a city and brought out his daughter and said: all the guests who come here shall not see my face until they first see the face of the matrona. So the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: you shall not bring a sacrifice before Me until a Sabbath has passed over it, for there is no week without Sabbath, and there is no circumcision without Sabbath — thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound. And the matrona is the final ה and it is first in the thought of the one who offers, and this is self-evident."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "Comment appears to cite Hosea 7:1 (״והמה כאדם עברו ברית״) with the label '(שם ו) — the preceding verse'; the verse is Hosea 7:1 in the Hebrew verse numbering (the sixth verse of the chapter beginning at 7:1)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:58.021516551Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}